---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
---

<Layout title="About" description="What Conchquest does: the Shelling Score, community find map, shell library, and more.">
  <Nav />

  <div class="wrap page-wrap">
    <div class="eyebrow">About Conchquest</div>
    <h1>Built for the walk to the beach, not just the app store page.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Conchquest started from a simple frustration: you drive to the beach, the tide's wrong, and you spend an hour
      finding nothing but broken fragments. We built the app we wanted — one that tells you when to go, what to
      expect, and what you're holding once you find it.
    </p>

    <h2>The Shelling Score</h2>
    <p>
      Every beach gets a single 0–100 score, rebuilt from live tide height, wind speed and direction, wave height,
      and moon phase. It's not a guess — it's the same handful of conditions experienced shellers already watch for,
      combined into one number so you don't have to check four different sites before you leave the house. The score
      also points to the best window in the day to be on the sand, not just whether today is good in the abstract.
    </p>

    <h2>Shelling Strategy</h2>
    <p>
      Alongside the score, Conchquest generates a short, plain-language strategy for your beach and conditions —
      where to look, what the tide is doing, and what to expect given the day's wind and wave setup. It reads like
      advice from someone who was just there, because it's built from the same forecast data as your score.
    </p>

    <h2>Multi-day forecast</h2>
    <p>
      Shelling conditions shift day to day, so Conchquest doesn't stop at today's snapshot. A multi-day forecast
      strip lets you plan a trip a few days out and pick the best day in a stretch, not just check the app the
      morning of.
    </p>

    <h2>Community find map</h2>
    <p>
      See what other shellers have been finding nearby — from common sand dollars to a rare Junonia — shared as they
      log their own finds. Every find you log is either public (shown to the community at its exact location) or
      private (visible only to you) — you choose per find.
    </p>

    <h2>Shell library</h2>
    <p>
      A curated field guide for identifying what's in your hand, with rarity, habitat, and ethical shelling guidance
      for every species — including what's legal to collect and what's better left on the beach.
    </p>

    <h2>Your shelling history</h2>
    <p>
      Every find you log builds a personal shelling history on your profile — species, condition, and where and when
      you found it. Save your regular beaches, set an alert threshold for each one, and Conchquest will notify you
      when conditions there cross into "go now" territory.
    </p>

    <h2>Where we're launching</h2>
    <p>
      Conchquest is launching first on Sanibel, Captiva, Bowman's Beach, and Marco Island — with more of the Gulf
      Coast to follow.
    </p>
  </div>

  <Footer />
</Layout>

<style>
  .page-wrap {
    max-width: 720px;
    padding: 56px 32px 100px;
  }
  .eyebrow {
    font-size: 12px;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--teal-deep);
    margin-bottom: 18px;
  }
  h1 {
    font-family: 'Fraunces', serif;
    font-size: 36px;
    font-weight: 600;
    margin-bottom: 20px;
    line-height: 1.2;
  }
  .lede {
    font-size: 17px;
    color: var(--muted);
    line-height: 1.7;
    margin-bottom: 12px;
  }
  h2 {
    font-family: 'Fraunces', serif;
    font-size: 20px;
    font-weight: 600;
    margin: 36px 0 12px;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  p {
    font-size: 15px;
    color: #3c3c39;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }
</style>
